"april99 (https://www.vos.noaa.gov/MWL/apr1999.pdf)\nciteturn24view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 108; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn22view0\",\"lineno\":850}); Total lines: 6538\nL759@P12: response).\nL760@P12: • AMVER message traffic is free via U.S. Coast Guard or coastal radio stations listed in the AMVER\nL761@P12: Bulletin (regular INMARSAT tariffs apply to AMVER traffic).\nL762@P12: • The AMVER center cannot acknowledge receipt of your transmission (no outgoing communications\nL763@P12: capability).\nL764@P12: • AMVER reports satisfy the 24-hour notice of arrival required under 33 CFR 160.207 and 160.209.\nL765@P12: • AMVER information is protected and released only to search and rescue authorities, and only in a\nL766@P12: bonafide emergency.\nL767@P12: • AMVER award eligibility after 128 days on plot in a year: blue pennant for 1-5 years, gold pennant for\nL768@P12: 6-10 years, purple pennant for over 10 years, plaque at 15 years, engraved pewter plate at 20 years.\nL769@P12: The more ships on the plot, the better the AMVER system works!\nL770@P12: Help AMVER grow!\nL771@P12-13: Please pass this information along to your fellow mariners!14 Mariners Weather Log\nL772@P13-14: AMVERApril 1999 15\nL773@P14: AMVER\nL774@P14-15: GMDSS Operating Guidance for Masters of Ships in Distress Situations16 Mariners Weather Log\nL775@P15-16: AMVERApril 1999 17\nL776@P16: Physical Oceanography\nL777@P16: Dr. Parker is Chief of the Coast\nL778@P16: Survey Development Laboratory\nL779@P16: in the National Ocean Service,\nL780@P16: NOAA.\nL781@P16: Nothing embodies the\nL782@P16: drama of the sea more\nL783@P16: than waves. To the\nL784@P16: average person, the images evoked\nL785@P16: are probably those of huge waves\nL786@P16: crashing onto a beach during a\nL787@P16: storm. To the recreational boater,\nL788@P16: it may be remembering the jarring\nL789@P16: rhythm of the boat’s bow banging\nL790@P16: down onto the next set of wave\nL791@P16: crests and the resulting spray as\nL792@P16: you headed for the comfort and\nL793@P16: dryness of the dock. To the\nL794@P16: mariner, it may be the memory of\nL795@P16: tons of water crashing onto the\nL796@P16: deck of the cargo ship, and\nL797@P16: perhaps of fear as the captain kept\nL798@P16: How Does the Wind Generate Waves?\nL799@P16: Bruce Parker\nL800@P16: changing course and speed in an\nL801@P16: effort to keep the vessel in a\nL802@P16: position where a huge wave could\nL803@P16: not turn it over or break it in two.\nL804@P16: Though it has always been obvi\u0002ous that waves were caused by the\nL805@P16: wind, it may be surprising to learn\nL806@P16: that we still do not completely\nL807@P16: understand how the wind blowing\nL808@P16: over a smooth flat water surface\nL809@P16: can generate waves and how these\nL810@P16: waves can sometimes grow to\nL811@P16: heights of 50 or even 100 feet.\nL812@P16: When oceanographers say some\u0002thing is “not completely under\u0002stood,” what they really mean is\nL813@P16: that they don’t yet have a reliable\nL814@P16: mathematical model that can\nL815@P16: accurately predict what will\nL816@P16: happen in the real world for all\nL817@P16: conditions. Even when a math\u0002ematical model does work well, it\nL818@P16: may not be easy to translate the\nL819@P16: mathematics of the model into\nL820@P16: physical terms that are easily\nL821@P16: described and understood. That\nL822@P16: being said, in this column we will\nL823@P16: try to explain how the wind\nL824@P16: generates waves, hopefully in\nL825@P16: physical terms that make sense.\nL826@P16: Let’s start with the most basic\nL827@P16: idea, i.e., the idea of a wave. In\nL828@P16: previous columns we have talked\nL829@P16: about an oscillation, such as a\nL830@P16: pendulum oscillating back and\nL831@P16: forth. A wave is merely an oscilla\u0002tion that does not stay in one\nL832@P16: place, but moves along or through\nL833@P16: some medium—for example,\nL834@P16: along a string, through the air, or\nL835@P16-17: Continued on Page 1818 Mariners Weather Log\nL836@P17: Physical Oceanography\nL837@P17: along the water’s surface (there\nL838@P17: are many kinds of waves in\nL839@P17: nature). If a taut string is plucked,\nL840@P17: and a point on that string moves\nL841@P17: up and down, that point is oscillat\u0002ing, but if the up and down motion\nL842@P17: moves along the string away from\nL843@P17: the point where it was plucked,\nL844@P17: that is a wave.\nL845@P17: For an oscillation or a wave to\nL846@P17: occur there must be a motionless\nL847@P17: at-rest position where all the\nL848@P17: forces are in balance (in equilib\u0002rium), and, when we upset this\nL849@P17: balance, there must be a restoring\nL850@P17: force that will try to bring it back\nL851@P17: to the equilibrium position.\nL852@P17: Suppose we have a ball attached\nL853@P17: to a string hanging motionless\nL854@P17: (i.e., a pendulum). We hit the ball\nL855@P17: to the left, and its inertia carries\nL856@P17: the ball further to the left and the\nL857@P17: string forces it to move upward\nL858@P17: against the force of gravity\nL859@P17: (Figure 1). Gravity, the restoring\nL860@P17: force, eventually slows the ball\nL861@P17: down until it stops at the maxi\u0002mum height of its swing, and then\nL862@P17: pulls it downward again. The ball\nL863@P17: moves back to the right toward the\nL864@P17: original point of equilibrium (the\nL865@P17: point where it was when originally\nL866@P17: motionless). However, with little\nL867@P17: friction to stop it, the ball’s inertia\nL868@P17: carries it right past this equilib\u0002rium point, moving to the right\nL869@P17: and once again upward against the\nL870@P17: force of gravity, which again\nL871@P17: slows it down and pulls it down\nL872@P17: and back to the left. Because there\nL873@P17: is very little friction in this system\nL874@P17: (mostly the friction of the air on\nL875@P17: the ball moving through it), the\nL876@P17: ball comes to a complete stop at\nL877@P17: the equilibrium position only after\nL878@P17: many oscillations.\nL879@P17: Water sitting in a tank (or in an\nL880@P17: ocean basin) with no external\nL881@P17: forces pushing on its surface, will\nL882@P17: also be in an equilibrium position,\nL883@P17: i.e., motionless with a flat surface.\nL884@P17: If something moves the water\nL885@P17: away from this flat equilibrium\nL886@P17: position, there are two restoring\nL887@P17: forces that will work to make the\nL888@P17: water surface flat again: surface\nL889@P17: tension and gravity. If only a very\nL890@P17: small part of the water surface\nL891@P17: (less than an inch) is bent (and\nL892@P17: stretched), then it will be flattened\nL893@P17: out again by the water’s surface\nL894@P17: tension (due to the attraction of\nL895@P17: the water molecules for each\nL896@P17: other). If a larger portion of the\nL897@P17: water surface is bent, and a\nL898@P17: portion of water is moved verti\u0002cally above the equilibrium level\nL899@P17: (above the original flat surface),\nL900@P17: gravity will pull the water back\nL901@P17: down. If the water is pushed down\nL902@P17: below the equilibrium level,\nL903@P17: causing a depression in the\nL904@P17: surface, then water pressure will\nL905@P17: force it back up. This water\nL906@P17: pressure is due to the gravity\nL907@P17: pulling down on the water around\nL908@P17: the depression. In all these cases,\nL909@P17: once the water starts moving back\nL910@P17: toward the equilibrium position,\nL911@P17: the inertia of the water will carry\nL912@P17: the surface past the equilibrium\nL913@P17: level, there being little friction to\nL914@P17: slow it down right away.\nL915@P17: But in this case, the water surface\nL916@P17: does not just go up and down at\nL917@P17: that one location. This up and\nL918@P17: How Wind Generates Waves\nL919@P17: Continued from page 17\nL920@P17-18: Figure 1. A simple oscillating pendulum. See text for explanation. Continued on Page 19April 1999 19\nL921@P18: Physical Oceanography\nL922@P18: down motion of the surface\nL923@P18: propagates as a wave away from\nL924@P18: that location. The reason the\nL925@P18: change in shape of the water\nL926@P18: surface (i.e., the wave) moves\nL927@P18: along the water surface away from\nL928@P18: the location where the original\nL929@P18: disturbance took place, is that\nL930@P18: water particles were also pushed\nL931@P18: horizontally, in addition to verti\u0002cally, forward and then backward.\nL932@P18: Individual water particles oscillate\nL933@P18: about their own equilibrium\nL934@P18: positions. As they interact with\nL935@P18: their neighboring particles they\nL936@P18: transfer some energy to them.\nL937@P18: Those particles in turn interact\nL938@P18: with other neighboring particles\nL939@P18: and transfer energy to them, and\nL940@P18: so on (Figure 2). In fact, these\nL941@P18: water particles actually oscillate in\nL942@P18: two dimensions, moving in\nL943@P18: (almost) perfect vertical circles\nL944@P18: (usually referred to as particle\nL945@P18: orbits). After a complete cycle of\nL946@P18: the wave, each particle comes\nL947@P18: back to (almost) where it started\nL948@P18: one cycle earlier. This is clear\nL949@P18: when a float is on the water\nL950@P18: surface. When a wave goes by, the\nL951@P18: float moves forward as it moves\nL952@P18: upward, and then it moves back\u0002ward as it moves downward. One\nL953@P18: also notes in Figure 2 that the\nL954@P18: wave has an effect on the water\nL955@P18: column below it. The circular\nL956@P18: particle orbits become smaller as\nL957@P18: one goes deeper, disappearing at a\nL958@P18: location of no wave motion at a\nL959@P18: depth equal to approximately half\nL960@P18: the wavelength. (If the water is too\nL961@P18: shallow for a location of no wave\nL962@P18: motion to occur, this shallow\u0002water wave will have elliptical\nL963@P18: particle orbits, as well as other\nL964@P18: different characteristics, which we\nL965@P18: will look at in a later Physical\nL966@P18: Oceanography column.)\nL967@P18: A key point to remember is that it\nL968@P18: is the shape of the water surface\nL969@P18: (and the energy) that is propagat\u0002ing away, not the water particles\nL970@P18: themselves. [“(almost)” was used\nL971@P18: twice in the previous paragraph\nL972@P18: because, to be very precise, there\nL973@P18: is a very, very small transport of\nL974@P18: water forward with each wave\nL975@P18: cycle, but this is insignificantly\nL976@P18: small compared with the speed of\nL977@P18: the propagating shape of the\nL978@P18: surface.]\nL979@P18: At this point we should define\nL980@P18: some terms related to waves\nL981@P18: (some of which you probably have\nL982@P18: already seen before), which are\nL983@P18: illustrated in Figure 3. Note,\nL984@P18: however, that the wave shown in\nL985@P18: Figure 3 is an idealized wave (or\nL986@P18: one component making up a\nL987@P18: wave). The highest point that the\nL988@P18: water surface reaches is the crest\nL989@P18: of the wave; the lowest point is\nL990@P18: the trough. The difference from\nL991@P18: trough to crest is the wave height.\nL992@P18: The distance from one crest to the\nL993@P18: next crest, or from one trough to\nL994@P18: the next trough, is the wavelength.\nL995@P18: How Wind Generates Waves\nL996@P18: Continued from page 18\nL997@P18: Continued on Page 20\nL998@P18: Figure 2. A simple propagating water wave with the water particle orbits\nL999@P18: shown.\nL1000@P18: Figure 3. Terms describing a simple water wave. The wave period is the\nL1001@P18-19: time is takes for one complete wave cycle to pass by a point.20 Mariners Weather Log\nL1002@P19: Physical Oceanography\nL1003@P19: How Wind Generates Waves\nL1004@P19: Continued from Page 19\nL1005@P19: As the wave propagates by a\nL1006@P19: point, the time it takes one wave\nL1007@P19: cycle to pass by that point (i.e., the\nL1008@P19: time it takes between the first crest\nL1009@P19: passing the point and the second\nL1010@P19: crest passing that point) is called\nL1011@P19: the wave period. The inverse of\nL1012@P19: the period is the frequency of the\nL1013@P19: wave, i.e. how many cycles of this\nL1014@P19: wave pass by in a second. The\nL1015@P19: speed at which a wave travels, its\nL1016@P19: wave speed or celerity, is equal to\nL1017@P19: its wavelength divided by the\nL1018@P19: wave period. This is different\nL1019@P19: from, and much greater than, the\nL1020@P19: speed of individual water par\u0002ticles.\nL1021@P19: So how does the wind generate\nL1022@P19: waves and make them grow? It’s\nL1023@P19: obvious how the wind pushes on\nL1024@P19: the sails of a sailboat. But what\nL1025@P19: can the wind do to a glassy-calm\nL1026@P19: flat sea surface? How can the\nL1027@P19: wind move the water surface and\nL1028@P19: change its shape? There are two\nL1029@P19: possible ways. The first is through\nL1030@P19: the friction of the horizontally\u0002flowing air particles in the wind\nL1031@P19: rubbing against the water particles\nL1032@P19: in the water surface (this wind\nL1033@P19: stress is a force tangential to the\nL1034@P19: surface). However, when the wind\nL1035@P19: blows over any surface, including\nL1036@P19: water, the movement of the air\nL1037@P19: particles is not simply parallel to\nL1038@P19: the surface. The flow of air is\nL1039@P19: turbulent, meaning that there are\nL1040@P19: swirling eddies of various sizes\nL1041@P19: and thus chaotic vertical move\u0002ments of the air particles (which\nL1042@P19: produce a force perpendicular to\nL1043@P19: the water surface). The second\nL1044@P19: way that the air can move the\nL1045@P19: water surface is through the\nL1046@P19: vertical pressure of the air par\u0002ticles, either moving downward\nL1047@P19: (pushing on and lowering the\nL1048@P19: water surface) or moving upward\nL1049@P19: (creating a reduced pressure that\nL1050@P19: lifts the water surface). The\nL1051@P19: question then is what role does\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFLIP Definition & Meaning - Merriam-Webster (https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/flip)\nciteturn24view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn22view2\",\"lineno\":28}); Total lines: 402\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com cite1†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: \nL2: [Input: Search Dictionary]\nL3: \nL4:   * cite2†Games L5:   * cite3†Word of the Day L6:   * cite4†Grammar L7:   * cite5†Word Finder L8:   * cite6†Slang L9:   * cite7†Newsletters L10:   * cite8†Wordplay L11:   * cite9†Rhymes L12:   * cite10†Thesaurus L13:   * cite11†Join MWU†unabridged.merriam-webster.com L14:   * More\nL15:     * cite2†Games L16:     * cite3†Word of the Day L17:     * cite4†Grammar L18:     * cite8†Wordplay L19:     * cite6†Slang L20:     * cite9†Rhymes L21:     * cite5†Word Finder L22:     * cite7†Newsletters L23:     * cite10†Thesaurus L24:     * cite11†Join MWU†unabridged.merriam-webster.com L25:     * Shop\nL26:     * cite12†Books†shop.merriam-webster.com L27:     * cite13†Merch†merriamwebster.threadless.com L28: \nL29: cite14†Dictionary Definition\nL30: \nL31:   * # flip\nL32: \nL33: 1 of 3\nL34: ## cite15†verb L35: \nL36: cite16†ˈflip  L37: \nL38: flipped; flipping; flips\nL39: \nL40: cite17†Synonyms of flip L41: \nL42: Simple Definition A Simple Definition is available from our Learner's Dictionary to help you understand the meaning faster.\nL43: \nL44: cite18†transitive verb L45: \nL46: 1\nL47: \nL48: : to toss so as to cause to turn over in the air\nL49: \nL50: flip a coin\nL51: \nL52: flip a pancake\nL53: \nL54: also : cite19†toss L55: \nL56: flip me the ball\nL57: \nL58: flip one end of the scarf over your shoulder\nL59: \nL60: 2\nL61: \nL62: a\nL63: \nL64: : to cause to turn and especially to turn over\nL65: \nL66: flipped the car\nL67: \nL68: flipping the pages of a book\nL69: \nL70: b\nL71: : to move with a small quick motion\nL72: \nL73: flip a switch\nL74: \nL75: c\nL76: \nL77: : to turn (something) on or off with a switch\nL78: \nL79: flip on/off the lights\nL80: \nL81: d\nL82: \nL83: : to change or move through (channels, stations, etc.) quickly\nL84: \nL85: flipping channels with the remote control\nL86: \nL87: 3\nL88: \nL89: : to buy and usually renovate (real estate) so as to quickly resell at a higher price\nL90: \nL91: He's flipped three houses for a substantial profit.\nL92: \nL93: 4\nL94: \nL95: : to cause or persuade (a witness) to cooperate in prosecuting a criminal case against an associate\nL96: … wrote that the … probe seemed to have reached the stage where investigators are in the process of flipping witnesses.—Nancy LeTourneau\nL97: \nL98: cite20†intransitive verb L99: \nL100: 1\nL101: \nL102: : to make a twitching or flicking movement\nL103: \nL104: The fish flipped and flopped on the deck.\nL105: \nL106: 2\nL107: \nL108: a\nL109: \nL110: : to turn or roll from one side to the other : cite21†turn over L111: \nL112: The crab had flipped onto its back.\nL113: \nL114: The car flipped over.\nL115: \nL116: b\nL117: \nL118: : to do a cite22†somersault in the air : to do a flip\nL119: \nL120: He flipped off the diving board.\nL121: \nL122: 3\nL123: \nL124: a\nL125: : to change from one state, position, subject, etc., to another\nL126: \nL127: The job market flipped from hot to cold.\nL128: \nL129: The commercial flipped back and forth between scenes of country life and city life.\nL130: \nL131: b\nL132: \nL133: : to change or move through channels, pages, etc.\nL134: \nL135: He sat on the couch flipping through the channels.\nL136: \nL137: flip to another station\nL138: \nL139: flip through the pages of a magazine\nL140: \nL141: c\nL142: \nL143: : to cooperate in the prosecution of a criminal case against an associate\nL144: It was only after Jones \"flipped\" and started testifying against Doe that defense counsel wanted to impeach him …—In re Sealed Case No. 99-3096, 185 F.3d 887, 1999\nL145: \nL146: Before sentencing, two more members defected … . And, soon after receiving his 45-year sentence in May 1989, Leonetti flipped, too.—Marguerite Del Giudice\nL147: \nL148: 4\nL149: \nL150: slang\nL151: \nL152: a\nL153: \nL154: : to lose one's reason or composure\nL155: \nL156: She completely flipped when she heard about her son's accident.\nL157: \nL158: see also cite23†flip one's lid , cite24†flip out L159: \nL160: b\nL161: : to become very enthusiastic\nL162: \nL163: see also:\nL164: \nL165: cite25†flip the script L166: \nL167: flip\nL168: \nL169: 2 of 3\nL170: ## cite26†noun L171: \nL172: 1\nL173: \nL174: : a mixed drink usually consisting of a sweetened spiced liquor with beaten eggs\nL175: \nL176: 2\nL177: \nL178: : an act or instance of cite27†flipping L179: \nL180: 3\nL181: \nL182: : the motion used in flipping\nL183: \nL184: 4\nL185: \nL186: : a somersault especially in the air\nL187: \nL188: 5\nL189: \nL190: : a holder for a collectible coin made of vinyl or cardboard with a plastic window and often having a sleeve in which to place an identifying insert\nL191: \nL192: flip\nL193: \nL194: 3 of 3\nL195: \nL196: ## cite28†adjective L197: \nL198: : cite29†flippant , cite30†impertinent L199: ## Synonyms of flip\nL200: \nL201: Relevance\nL202: \nL203: Verb\nL204: \nL205:   * cite31†skim L206:   * cite32†thumb L207:   * cite33†leaf L208: \nL209: Adjective\nL210: \nL211:   * cite34†cute L212:   * cite35†wise L213:   * cite36†playful L214: \nL215: cite17†See All Synonyms & Antonyms in Thesaurus L216: ## Examples of flip in a Sentence\nL217: \nL218: Verb She was sitting in the waiting room, flipping the pages of a magazine. His car flipped over on the interstate. Noun the flip of a coin She turned on the lights with the flip of a switch. Adjective made some flip comment about the marriage between the old man and the considerably younger woman\nL219: \nL220: Recent Examples on the Web\nL221: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. cite14†Read More Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. cite37†Send us feedback†docs.google.com .\nL222: \nL223: Verb\nL224: With the genre’s scarcity on the charts in 2026 — so far, Drake is the only rapper to notch a top 10 hit on the Hot 100 this year as a lead artist — flipping a once-big hit might seem like an easy remedy. — Michael Saponara, Billboard, 5 Oct. 2026 In a world where that happens, the huge number of bearish stocks would flip instantly. — Jim Cramer, CNBC, 4 Oct. 2026\nL225: \nL226: Noun\nL227: In 2011, Joe Kay debuted Soulection as a college radio show at Cal State Long Beach, spinning rare B-sides, progressive beats, inventive flips of classic records and soulful underground tracks. — Kailyn Brown, Los Angeles Times, 3 Oct. 2026 Many pounds of food stored overnight in the flip-top cooler and keg cooler got sent to the trash by a Stop Sale for temperature abuse. — David J. Neal, Miami Herald, 2 Oct. 2026 cite38†See All Example Sentences for flip L228: ## Word History\nL229: \nL230: Etymology\nL231: \nL232: Verb, Noun, and Adjective\nL233: \nL234: probably imitative\nL235: \nL236: First Known Use\nL237: \nL238: Verb\nL239: \nL240: 1616, in the meaning defined at cite39†transitive sense 1 L241: \nL242: Noun\nL243: \nL244: 1611, in the meaning defined at cite40†sense 2 L245: \nL246: Adjective\nL247: \nL248: 1846, in the meaning defined cite41†above L249: \nL250: Time Traveler\nL251: \nL252: The first known use of flip was in 1611\nL253: \nL254: cite42†See more words from the same year L255: \nL256: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL257: \nL258: cite43†f